{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"13f06aa5\" lines=\"36-51\">\n<description>\nExtend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.\n- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.\n\nRule:\n- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.\n- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.\n- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.\n\nKey insight:\n- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"95755ff2\" lines=\"45-60\">\n<description>\nPaint straight rays from colored side markers into the hollow diamond frame until an interior foreground pixel blocks each ray.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains one complete one-pixel-thick diamond frame.\n- Colored marker pixels outside the diamond sit on the top, bottom, left, or right side of the frame's bounding box.\n- Any non-background, non-frame pixels inside the diamond are blockers that remain unchanged.\n\nRule\n- Each outside marker emits inward perpendicular to its side of the diamond bounding box.\n- Along that row or column, paint only background pixels strictly inside the diamond with the marker color.\n- Stop a ray when it reaches an existing interior foreground pixel; keep the frame and all original colored pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The diamond frame defines a strict interior, while the side markers are column or row emitters whose color fills the visible interior interval until blocked.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d35bdbdc\" lines=\"120-134\">\n<description>\nTransfer center colors between bordered sprites matched by color, keeping only path-connected ones.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, grey (5) path network, and several small \"bordered sprites\" each having a border color and a single center pixel of a different color.\n- Grey path forms a one-stroke trail under 8-connectivity; its start/end cells touch exactly two destination sprites via ports.\n\nRule:\n- Destination sprites are determined by the grey trail's two endpoints.\n- For each destination, find the source sprite whose border color equals the destination's center color.\n- Replace the destination's center pixel with the source's center color.\n- Delete all non-destination sprites; grey paths and destinations remain.\n\nKey insight: Center color of each destination acts as a lookup key into the border colors of other sprites, enabling indirect color transfer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7acdf6d3\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nMove the source-color pixels into the unique outline interior whose size matches the source count.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms 8-connected outline objects with row-wise interior cells.\n- The other foreground color supplies a set of source pixels away from the chosen outline.\nRule\n- For each possible outline color, compute each 8-connected outline object's\n  row-span interior: background pixels strictly between the leftmost and\n  rightmost outline pixels on each occupied row.\n- Select the unique outline interior whose cell count equals the total\n  number of pixels of the other foreground color.\n- Erase the source pixels and paint that matched interior with the source color.\nKey insight\n- The colors are roles, not fixed IDs: the source color is identified by\n  counting, and the destination is the outline with a matching interior area.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bd5af378\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nRewrite a corner L block into two nested L borders and a diagonal trace.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid has exactly two colors: one color forms a one-cell-thick\nL on two adjacent outer edges, and the other color fills the remaining\nrectangle.\n\nRule\nNormalize the L to the top-left corner. Paint the outer top-left L with the\nfill color, the next inner L with the original border color, and the main\ndiagonal of the remaining inner rectangle with the fill color. All other\ncells become color 8, then the original orientation is restored.\n\nKey insight\nThe corner of the input L determines the orientation; after flipping to a\ncanonical top-left L, the output is the same nested-layer pattern for every\nexample.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"396d80d7\" lines=\"18-30\">\n<description>\nPaint the inner foreground color into every background pixel that touches the enclosing frame only diagonally.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color is an enclosing frame: its bbox strictly contains the bbox of the other, inner color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep all existing pixels unchanged.\n- For each background pixel, if it is diagonally adjacent to the frame color and has no orthogonal adjacency to the frame color, recolor it to the inner color.\n\nKey insight: The inner color marks the color to use, while the frame's diagonal-only background neighbors define the cells to add.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"984d8a3e\" lines=\"5-21\">\n<description>\nShift each row's dominant middle-color run left until its right edge aligns with the leftmost original right edge, moving displaced left-side pixels after the run.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has three colors.\n- The most common color forms one contiguous horizontal run in every row.\n- Pixels before those runs share one side color, and pixels after those runs share the other side color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the minimum right edge column among the middle-color runs.\n- In each row, keep the run width and the trailing right-side color count.\n- Place the middle-color run so its right edge is at that minimum column, unless the run is too wide, in which case start it at column 0.\n- Fill any remaining non-trailing cells with the left-side color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe varying row prefixes are movable padding; the stable feature is the leftmost right boundary of the middle-color shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\" lines=\"97-117\">\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4a21e3da\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\n\nRule:\n- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).\n- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.\n- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.\n\nKey insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
